1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:03,520
Here's a balloon, blown up at room temperature.

2
00:00:03,820 --> 00:00:07,934
Put it in the freezer for an hour, and it comes out noticeably smaller.

3
00:00:08,234 --> 00:00:11,161
Leave it on the bench, and it slowly puffs back up.

4
00:00:11,461 --> 00:00:13,462
No air got in or out.

5
00:00:13,512 --> 00:00:15,333
Same balloon, same air.

6
00:00:15,383 --> 00:00:17,518
So what's going on inside?

7
00:00:19,268 --> 00:00:24,120
Everything around you, solids, liquids and gases, is made of tiny particles.

8
00:00:24,420 --> 00:00:27,226
We'll draw each particle as a simple circle.

9
00:00:27,526 --> 00:00:29,697
And these particles are never still.

10
00:00:29,747 --> 00:00:32,161
They're in constant, random motion.

11
00:00:32,461 --> 00:00:35,928
Follow just one, and its path is a jumble of zigzags.

12
00:00:35,978 --> 00:00:39,525
It changes direction every time it bumps into a neighbour.

13
00:00:41,275 --> 00:00:44,605
The particle model explains the three states of matter.

14
00:00:44,905 --> 00:00:48,390
In a solid, particles are packed tightly in fixed positions.

15
00:00:48,690 --> 00:00:50,517
But they're not stationary.

16
00:00:50,567 --> 00:00:53,377
Each one vibrates on the spot, all the time.

17
00:00:53,677 --> 00:00:58,418
In a liquid, particles are still close together, but they can slide past each other.

18
00:00:58,468 --> 00:01:00,266
That's why liquids flow.

19
00:01:00,566 --> 00:01:03,029
In a gas, particles are far apart.

20
00:01:03,079 --> 00:01:06,529
They move fast, in straight lines, until they hit something.

21
00:01:06,829 --> 00:01:09,250
And the space between gas particles?

22
00:01:09,300 --> 00:01:10,496
It's empty.

23
00:01:10,546 --> 00:01:11,672
Not air.

24
00:01:11,722 --> 00:01:13,119
Nothing at all.

25
00:01:13,169 --> 00:01:14,987
The particles are the air.

26
00:01:16,737 --> 00:01:18,453
Now let's heat a gas.

27
00:01:18,753 --> 00:01:22,134
As the temperature rises, the particles move faster.

28
00:01:22,434 --> 00:01:25,442
This graph shows how many particles have each speed.

29
00:01:25,492 --> 00:01:28,576
As we heat the gas, the whole graph shifts to the right.

30
00:01:28,876 --> 00:01:33,293
Temperature is a measure of the average kinetic energy of the particles.

31
00:01:33,343 --> 00:01:35,634
Hotter means faster, on average.

32
00:01:35,934 --> 00:01:39,656
Notice what doesn't change: the size of each particle.

33
00:01:39,706 --> 00:01:42,080
Particles don't expand when heated.

34
00:01:42,130 --> 00:01:43,690
They just speed up.

35
00:01:45,440 --> 00:01:48,897
Gas particles keep crashing into the walls of their container.

36
00:01:48,947 --> 00:01:51,427
Watch the walls light up with every hit.

37
00:01:51,727 --> 00:01:53,779
Each hit is a tiny push.

38
00:01:53,829 --> 00:01:57,126
Countless pushes every second add up to gas pressure.

39
00:01:57,426 --> 00:02:01,276
Heat the gas, and the particles hit the walls more often, and harder.

40
00:02:01,576 --> 00:02:04,316
More hits, and harder hits, mean higher pressure.

41
00:02:06,066 --> 00:02:07,856
So, back to the balloon.

42
00:02:08,156 --> 00:02:11,371
In the freezer, the air particles inside slow down.

43
00:02:11,421 --> 00:02:14,056
They hit the rubber less often, and less hard.

44
00:02:14,356 --> 00:02:20,742
The push from inside drops below the push from the air outside, so the balloon shrinks until the pushes balance.

45
00:02:21,042 --> 00:02:22,952
Same number of particles.

46
00:02:23,002 --> 00:02:24,880
Same size particles.

47
00:02:24,930 --> 00:02:26,285
Just slower.

48
00:02:26,585 --> 00:02:30,747
Warm it up, the particles speed up again, and the balloon puffs back out.

49
00:02:32,497 --> 00:02:33,842
Let's recap.

50
00:02:34,142 --> 00:02:35,202
One.

51
00:02:35,252 --> 00:02:40,266
All matter is made of particles in constant, random motion, even in solids.

52
00:02:40,566 --> 00:02:41,350
Two.

53
00:02:41,400 --> 00:02:46,564
Solids, liquids and gases differ in how close the particles are, and how they move.

54
00:02:46,864 --> 00:02:47,692
Three.

55
00:02:47,742 --> 00:02:51,592
Temperature measures the average kinetic energy of the particles.

56
00:02:51,642 --> 00:02:54,094
Heating makes them faster, not bigger.
